4. Disconnect the battery: To prevent any electrical shock, disconnect the negative (-) terminal from the battery first.

5. Jump the ignition wires: Connect the two ignition wires together. This will bypass the ignition switch and send power directly to the starter motor.

6. Jump the battery: Connect the negative (-) terminal back to the battery, but this time to the same spot where you connected the ignition wires. This will ground the circuit and complete the path for the electricity to flow.

7. Start the car: With the key out of the ignition, try to start the car. If you've connected everything correctly, the car should start.

8. Reconnect the battery: Once the car is started, reconnect the negative (-) terminal to its original position on the battery. Then, reconnect the positive (+) terminal.

